A||||7||Zoom88||Rear trunk latch wiring ....HELP !!||||5||
Z||000000||Zoom88||11-19-2001||12:37 PM||||Fiero Rear Electric Trunk Latch.<BR>I have a Rear Electric Trunk Latch and switch. I found that the connections for the switch are already factory installed. My question is does anyone know if the wire was run to the engine bay area and if so how do I determine which one it is ?<BR> If I keep these questions up....maybe, one day I will be as sharp as you guess !!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||11-19-2001||01:25 PM||||If I'm not mistaken, the existing wiring harness in the car will already include the wiring from the switch to the relay under the steering wheel, and all the way back to the passenger-side decklid hinge. It's a 4-prong weatherproof plug, usually wiretied or clipped to the side of the hinge bracket. (At least, this is how it has been for the half-dozen Fieros I've worked on.)<P>You will need all of these items in order to install a working system - <BR>- Dash switch<BR>- Relay (socket is under the dash, near the steering column)<BR>- Decklid wiring harness inside stiff plastic sheath<BR>- Latch solenoid or latch with solenoid already attached<P>If your car came without the electric decklid release, there is still a wiring harness inside your decklid - it runs to the 'Door Ajar' light on the instrument panel. You need to unplug this harness from the latch and the weather proof plug, and pull it out of the decklid. You then need to insert the new wiring harness back through the decklid (the stiff plastic tubing makes this *much* easier than I pictured it being).<P>But yes, the wiring harness is already run from the under-dash relay to the socket beside the decklid hinge.  <P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
